@startuml transpose-two-arc-chain
' D · Damerau transposition "ba" → "ab" as a two-arc chain through a dedicated Tr state.
' arc1 substitutes (cost 1) into the transposition state; arc2 matches (cost 0) back onto the band.
' Net cost of the whole transposition = 1.
skinparam backgroundColor #FFFFFF
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName "DejaVu Sans"
skinparam state {
  BackgroundColor #D6EAF8
  BorderColor #34495E
  FontName "DejaVu Sans"
}
title Transposition as a two-arc chain — "ba" (query) <latex>\to</latex> "ab" (dictionary)\ninput tape = query   ·   output tape = dictionary   ·   total weight = 1

state "N(i, e)\nnormal band state" as N0
state "Tr(i, e+1)\ntransposition state\n(one edit already charged)" as TR #D6EAF8
state "N(i+2, e+1)\nback on the band" as N2

[*] -> N0
N0 -[#E74C3C]-> TR : <b>substitute</b>  <latex>q[i] : q[i{+}1]\ /\ 1</latex>\nemit the 2nd query char first
TR -[#2ECC71]-> N2 : <b>match</b>  <latex>q[i{+}1] : q[i]\ /\ 0</latex>\nemit the 1st query char, free
N2 -> [*]

note bottom of TR
  The pair reads query <latex>q[i]\,q[i{+}1] = </latex> "ba" and writes dictionary
  <latex>q[i{+}1]\,q[i] = </latex> "ab". Only arc 1 costs; arc 2 is free, so a single
  adjacent transposition is charged <b>one</b> edit (Damerau–Levenshtein),
  not two substitutions. The intermediate <b>Tr</b> state exists solely to
  sequence the swap and cannot be a final/accepting state.
end note
@enduml
